---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const chromeStoreUrl =
  "https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/solstice/boegcpnmpagdbebakchpambpfggghjhe";
const githubUrl = "https://github.com/seanoliver/solstice";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy policy — Solstice"
  description="How the Solstice Chrome extension handles settings, location, and third-party services."
>
  <header class="site-header">
    <nav class="nav container" aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Solstice home">
        <img src="/icon-128.png" alt="" width="30" height="30" />
        <span>Solstice</span>
      </a>
      <div class="nav-actions">
        <a class="text-link" href={githubUrl}>GitHub</a>
        <a class="button" href={chromeStoreUrl}>Add to Chrome</a>
      </div>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main class="policy-main">
    <article class="policy container">
      <p class="section-kicker">Plain-language policy</p>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated: August 9, 2026</p>

      <p>
        Solstice is a Chrome new-tab extension that displays a world clock. It
        has <strong>no servers, no accounts, no analytics, no tracking, and no advertising.</strong>
        The developer never receives any of your data.
      </p>
      <p>
        This document describes exactly what the extension does with
        information, because it requests location access and contacts a few
        third-party services. Everything below is verifiable in the source code.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
      <p>
        All of your settings are stored locally in your browser
        (<code>localStorage</code>) and never leave your machine:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your list of timezones and cities.</li>
        <li>Your 12h/24h preference and other view settings.</li>
        <li>Your manually entered “home” label, if you set one.</li>
        <li>
          The most recently detected city name and coordinates for your local
          card, cached for 24 hours to avoid repeated lookups and calculate
          local sunrise and sunset times.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Uninstalling the extension, or clearing its site data, removes all of it.</p>

      <h2>Location</h2>
      <p>To label your local card with a city name, the extension tries, in order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>A “home” label you typed yourself.</strong> This is fully
          local and takes priority. If you set it, no location detection or
          network request happens.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your browser's Geolocation.</strong> Only if you grant
          Chrome's permission. Your approximate coordinates are sent once to
          BigDataCloud's reverse-geocoding API to turn them into a city name.
          The city and coordinates are cached locally for 24 hours so Solstice
          can calculate daylight accurately without asking again. They are not
          sent anywhere else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Approximate IP-based location.</strong> Used only when
          Geolocation is denied or unavailable. Solstice requests your city
          from ipwho.is, ipapi.co, or get.geojs.io. These services can see your
          IP address as part of the request. The returned city and approximate
          coordinates are cached locally for 24 hours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your system time zone.</strong> A fully offline fallback if
          everything above fails.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Sunrise and sunset times for the day-night bands are computed locally
        from each zone's coordinates using an astronomical formula—no network
        request is involved.
      </p>

      <h2>Adding a city</h2>
      <p>
        When you search for a city to add, your search text is sent to
        Open-Meteo's free geocoding API to return matching cities and their
        coordinates. The query is sent only while you are actively typing in
        the add-city box.
      </p>

      <h2>Third-party services</h2>
      <p>These services are contacted only as described above:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.bigdatacloud.com/privacy-policy">BigDataCloud</a>
          — reverse geocoding
        </li>
        <li>ipwho.is, ipapi.co, and get.geojs.io — IP geolocation fallback</li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/terms">Open-Meteo</a> — city search
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Solstice sends them only the minimum needed for the feature: your
        coordinates, an implicit IP address, or your typed search query. It
        sends no identifier, account, or browsing history because it has none.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>geolocation</code></strong> — used solely to label your
          local card and calculate its daylight. You can deny it; Solstice
          falls back to IP or your time zone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Host permissions</strong> — required so the extension may
          contact the geolocation and geocoding services listed above.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions or concerns? Email
        <a href="mailto:solstice@seanoliver.dev">solstice@seanoliver.dev</a>
        or open an issue on the
        <a href={githubUrl}>GitHub repository</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <div class="footer-inner container">
      <span>© 2026 Sean Oliver · Open source under MIT</span>
      <div class="footer-links">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <a href={githubUrl}>GitHub</a>
        <a href="mailto:solstice@seanoliver.dev">Contact</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
